  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nRain from this morning is moving out of our area to the northeast \nwith drier conditions now expected for most of the area, though\nsome sprinkles could still fall in our northwestern areas. A \ncold front currently over the Sandhills will move southeast over\nour area this afternoon bringing steady north-northwesterly \nwinds that could gust 30-40 MPH and clearing the clouds. While \ntemps cool slightly behind the front, the steady winds prevents \nthe temps from falling underneath the frosting threshold \novernight for the majority of the area, with lows staying in the\nupper 30s-low 40s. A few portions in our north and west do drop\ninto the frost thresholds, however the steady winds will \nprevent frost from forming. \n\nThe steady north-northwesterly winds continue through the daytime \nFriday bringing cool air down from the north and keeping highs in \nthe low-mid 50s. Northern and eastern portions of the area will have \nsome clouds in the morning but they clear in the afternoon. After \nsunset the winds begin to subside and with clear skies, temperatures \nare able to cool much more efficiently than the night before. \nWidespread lows in the upper 20s-low 30s are possible which \nwould bring the first frost/freeze to the area. Southern \nportions of the area, especially north central KS, could stay \nwarm enough to prevent a full freeze however, most models keep \ntemps in the 30-34 range so the entire area is included in a \nFreeze Watch. \n\nThe upper level troughing that had brought the cooler air to the \narea begins to move east and a slight ridging pattern begins to \nsetup into the weekend. As the ridging pattern builds into the area, \ntemperatures will begin to warm again starting Saturday with highs \nreaching back into the upper 50s-low 60s. Temperatures continue to \nwarm into next week with highs reaching the upper 60s-70s starting \nSunday and could even flirt with 80 degrees in north central Kansas \nmid-week. Also, as the ridging persists over the area precip chances \nwill remain low, though some models have chances returning mid-week. \nAside from the low mid-week precip chances the area will largely \nremain dry through the end of next week.",
